Feature: Backup and vacuum of customer_360_raw table in Databricks

  As a Data Engineer
  I want to efficiently back up customer_360_raw table data as compressed parquet files partitioned by state
  And vacuum the original source table to retain only the last 30 days of records
  So that storage is optimized without compromising data retrieval or quality

  Background:
    Given the Databricks Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" exists
    And the Unity Catalog table is accessible with read permissions
    And the backup destination volume "Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup" exists and is writable
    And the customer_360_raw table has a column named "state" for partitioning
    And the retention column for the vacuum operation is "updated_at" of datatype TIMESTAMP in "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss" format
    And all columns are to be included in the backup
    And the backup parquet files must use Snappy compression
    And old Parquet backups in the backup volume are not deleted as part of this process
    And the script is run manually or as scheduled by external orchestration

  Scenario: Successful backup of customer_360_raw table as partitioned, compressed Parquet files
    Given the Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" contains records with valid "state" values (e.g., "CA", "NY", "TX")
    When the PySpark backup script is executed
    Then all records from "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" are written to Parquet files in "Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    And the backup files are partitioned by the "state" column
    And the backup files are compressed using Snappy compression
    And all columns from the source table are included in the backup
    And the Parquet files are validated for schema correctness and partitioning

  Scenario Outline: Error when backup volume is not available or writable
    Given the backup volume "<backup_volume>" does not exist or is not writable
    When the PySpark backup script is executed
    Then the script fails with error message "<error_message>"

    Examples:
      | backup_volume                      | error_message                                                       |
      | Volumes/missing_backup_volume      | Backup volume Volumes/missing_backup_volume does not exist.         |
      | Volumes/readonly_backup_volume     | Backup volume Volumes/readonly_backup_volume is not writable.       |

  Scenario: Error when customer_360_raw table is not accessible
    Given the Unity Catalog table "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" does not exist or is not accessible
    When the PySpark backup script is executed
    Then the script fails with error message "Source table purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw does not exist or is not accessible."

  Scenario Outline: Error when "state" column contains null or invalid values during backup
    Given the Unity Catalog table contains rows with invalid "state" values ("<state_value>")
    When the PySpark backup script attempts to partition by the "state" column
    Then the script moves records with null or empty "state" to the "__INVALID_STATE__" partition
    And logs a warning "Found records with null/blank 'state'; stored under __INVALID_STATE__ partition."

    Examples:
      | state_value |
      | null        |
      | ""          |

  Scenario: Successful vacuum of customer_360_raw table retaining last 30 days of records (based on updated_at field)
    Given the Unity Catalog table contains records with "updated_at" timestamp values
    When the Databricks VACUUM RETAIN operation is triggered for "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw"
    And the vacuum retains all records with "updated_at" >= (current_timestamp - interval 30 days)
    Then records older than 30 days based on the "updated_at" field are permanently deleted from the original table
    And no records with "updated_at" >= (current_timestamp - interval 30 days) are deleted

  Scenario: Error when "updated_at" column is missing or has incorrect datatype for vacuum operation
    Given the Unity Catalog table does not contain the "updated_at" column or it's not of TIMESTAMP type
    When the Databricks VACUUM operation is attempted
    Then the vacuum process fails with error message "Column 'updated_at' missing or not of type TIMESTAMP; cannot perform time-based vacuum operation."

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test for backup partitioning correctness
    Given the Unity Catalog table contains records with the following values:
      | customer_id | state | updated_at          |
      | 1001        | NY    | 2024-06-01 10:25:00 |
      | 1002        | TX    | 2024-05-15 14:30:00 |
      | 1003        | CA    | 2024-04-28 09:45:00 |
      | 1004        | NY    | 2024-05-31 16:18:00 |
    When the PySpark backup script is executed
    Then a directory "Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/state=<partition_state>" exists for each state value in the data
    And all records with state="<partition_state>" are written to the corresponding partition

    Examples:
      | partition_state |
      | NY              |
      | TX              |
      | CA              |

  Scenario: Parquet schema and data validation after backup
    Given the backup script has completed successfully
    When a schema inspection of one parquet file from the backup is performed
    Then the parquet schema must match the source table schema exactly
    And data values must match the source for all sampled records

  Scenario: Compression validation on backup Parquet files
    Given the backup files are created in the backup volume
    When the file properties are checked
    Then each Parquet file is compressed using Snappy codec

  Scenario: Validation that all columns are included in the Parquet
    Given the backup Parquet files are created
    When the columns of the output files are inspected
    Then all columns from "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw" are present in the backup files

  Scenario: Handling of large datasets during backup
    Given the source table contains more than 10 million records
    When the backup script is run
    Then the backup completes successfully without running out of storage or memory
    And partitioning is used to optimize write performance

  Scenario: Error when backup job does not have permission to read from source table
    Given the backup job's user or service account lacks read permission on "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw"
    When the backup job attempts to read from the table
    Then the job fails with error message "Permission denied: Unable to read from source table purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw."

  Scenario: Error when backup job does not have permission to write to backup volume
    Given the backup job's user or service account lacks write permission on "Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup"
    When the backup job attempts to write files
    Then the job fails with error message "Permission denied: Unable to write to backup volume Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup."

  Scenario: Logging and auditability
    Given the backup and vacuum operations are executed
    Then operation logs must include timestamp, operation type (backup/vacuum), affected partitions, record counts, and any error messages

  Scenario: Validation of vacuum grace period enforcement
    Given the vacuum operation is invoked with a retention period of 30 days (720 hours)
    When the vacuum is performed
    Then no records with updated_at newer than (current_timestamp - interval 30 days) are deleted
    And logs are created with details of number of records deleted and retained
